{"context":"The client must ship as a single binary to laptops, desktops and embedded devices spanning amd64 and arm64, run entirely in a terminal, and eventually carry real-time audio with a latency budget in the tens of milliseconds. The candidates were Go, Rust, and Python as a rapid-prototyping route. The binding constraint discovered during investigation is that no viable pure-Go Opus encoder exists, so any audio path requires a C dependency — which collides with the single-static-binary goal.","decision":"**Go, with CGO confined to the audio module behind a build tag.**\n\nEverything except audio is pure Go: the coordinator, the terminal UI, the WebSocket control plane, protobuf codecs, file transfer, and persistence — the last of these using a **pure-Go SQLite implementation** so that storage does not drag CGO into the default build.\n\nAudio is compiled only under the `voice` build tag, using a miniaudio binding for capture and playback and libopus for encode and decode.\n\nCross-compilation follows from this split:\n\n- **default build** — plain `GOOS`/`GOARCH`, no C toolchain, fully static, every target;\n- **audio build** — `zig cc` as the C compiler, which avoids maintaining a sysroot per ARM target.","consequences":"**Positive.** The default binary is a static pure-Go artifact that cross-compiles to every target with no toolchain setup, which directly serves the 'degrade rather than fail' principle: a device that cannot get libopus still gets a fully functional text, presence and file client. Go's TUI and WebSocket ecosystems are the most mature of the three candidates, and a single language covers both client and coordinator.\n\n**Negative.** There are now two build variants to produce, test and release, and the audio variant needs a C cross-compiler in CI. Contributors touching audio face CGO. And Go's garbage collector gives up the tight, predictable control over audio buffers that Rust would have provided.\n\n**The escape hatch, stated now rather than discovered later.** The GC concession is acceptable because stage 1 (voice notes) has no latency requirement at all, and stage 2's budget is dominated by the jitter buffer rather than by allocation. If measured stage-2 latency on ARM fails because of GC pauses, the response is to move the audio engine into a separate process — not to rewrite the tool. Recording that boundary now is what keeps this decision reversible at acceptable cost.","alternativesConsidered":"**Rust.** The strongest technical fit for the audio path: no GC pauses, precise buffer control, the smallest footprint — which matters on embedded devices. Rejected on delivery grounds rather than technical ones. Cross-compiling to the ARM targets needs additional tooling, the TUI layer is materially more manual work, and for an internal tool serving fewer than 20 devices the added development time buys latency headroom the MVP does not need. Note that Rust remains the natural choice if the audio engine is ever split into its own process.\n\n**Python.** Fastest route to a working demonstration of the protocol and the interaction model. Rejected outright on the deployment constraints: it fails the single-binary requirement, and shipping an interpreter plus native wheels to ARM embedded devices is heavier than the tool it would be delivering.\n\n**Go with a pure-Go audio path, avoiding CGO entirely.** Investigated and found unavailable: encoding requires libopus, and the pure-Go implementation covers decode only. Shipping raw uncompressed audio instead was rejected because it defeats the bandwidth constraint that motivated Opus in the first place."}